{"id":"billingconductor","corpus":"aws","title":"AWS Billing Conductor","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/billingconductor/latest/userguide/what-is-billingconductor.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"AWS Billing Conductor"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Billing Conductor is a custom billing service for AWS Channel Partners (Partners) and organizations that have chargeback requirements. For Partners, chargebacks are a prerequisite to getting paid by their customers. For direct customers, showback or, chargeback activities ensure that the business allocates the costs of a specific team, business unit, subsidiary or affiliate company, to the correct internal budget or profit and loss (P&L) statement."},{"kind":"p","text":"To achieve these activities, Billing Conductor enables users to create a second version of their costs to share with their customers or account owners. The second version of cloud cost data comes from your Billing Conductor configuration (pricing plans, pricing rules, and custom line items)."},{"kind":"p","text":"This data is called pro forma cost data, which represents costs for accounts in billing groups. These Billing Conductor resources can include a subset of accounts in an AWS Organizations (standard billing group) when using Billing Conductor as a standalone service, or an entire AWS Organizations (billing transfer billing group) when using Billing Conductor with billing transfer."},{"kind":"p","text":"Pro forma data exists in a separate domain (pro forma) from your billable cost data (standard billable). While billable cost data uses AWS-determined pricing, pro forma data is accessible through billing views."},{"kind":"p","text":"The primary billing view for accounts or AWS Organizations in billing groups shows only pro forma data. The account that configures the Billing Conductor pricing resources gains access to the billing view that shows the pro forma data displayed to accounts or AWS Organizations in billing groups."},{"kind":"p","text":"Customers will observe cost differences between billable costs (matching the AWS invoice) and pro forma costs (matching the Billing Conductor configuration) throughout the month. However, usage values will match at the end of each month, once the AWS invoice is issued."},{"kind":"p","text":"Defining pro forma costs enables customers to model their costs uniformly to match one of the following use cases:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Customer agreements, which can be a Partner use case negotiated outside of AWS"},{"kind":"li","text":"Internal accounting practices, often an organization-specific use case"},{"kind":"p","text":"Billing Conductor configurations don't affect customers' existing invoices from AWS or billing configurations (for example, sharing of credits or commitment-based discounts like Reserved Instances or Savings Plans)."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can analyze pro forma costs from either a management account of a standalone AWS Organizations or from a bill transfer account that manages multiple AWS Organizations bills."},{"kind":"li","text":"Analyze margins (the difference between pro forma costs and billable costs for the same set of accounts) within Billing Conductor"},{"kind":"li","text":"Use to analyze cost data using either billing group views or billing transfer views"},{"kind":"li","text":"Analyze cost data by using Billing Group views or Billing Transfer views on the Bills page"},{"kind":"li","text":"Create a for each Billing Group view and Billing Transfer view"},{"kind":"li","text":"View reservation and Savings Plans coverage and utilization reports that reflect Billing Group and Billing Transfer views"},{"kind":"p","text":"Billing Conductor managed accounts and AWS Organizations can analyze pro forma costs in AWS Cost Explorer, Cost and Usage Reports, the Billing dashboard, and the billing details page. Managed accounts and AWS Organizations in billing groups can create budgets to monitor their pro forma spend and receive alerts when they exceed, or are forecasted to exceed, their desired pro forma spending limit."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can configure billing groups, pricing plans, pricing rules, and custom line items in the Billing Conductor console or by using the Billing Conductor API."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information about AWS Billing Conductor service quotas, see Quotas and restrictions. For more information about billing transfer, see Transfer billing management to external accounts."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Sign up for an AWS account"},{"kind":"p","text":"To get started with AWS, you need an AWS account. For information about creating an AWS account, see Getting started with an AWS account in the AWS Account Management Reference Guide."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Features in AWS Billing Conductor"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can use the AWS Billing Conductor features to do the following:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Group accounts in standard billing groups"},{"kind":"p","text":"Organize accounts into Billing Conductor billing groups to view aggregated pro forma costs that model your AWS Organizations structure to reflect your business financial organization."},{"kind":"p","text":"Assign AWS Organizations to billing transfer billing group"},{"kind":"p","text":"When using billing transfer, you can use Billing Conductor to create billing groups that map one-to-one with an AWS Organizations so that the entire organization (including the management account) can view the pro forma costs data exclusively."}],"lemmas":{"billing":["billing","bill"],"billings":["billing","billings"],"billed":["billed","bill"],"conductors":["conductor","conductors"],"customs":["custom","customs"],"services":["service","services"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"channels":["channel","channels"],"channeling":["channeling","channel"],"channelled":["channelled","channel"],"channeled":["channeled","channel"],"partners":["partner","partners"]